ween angles at e and f. So |(b─c)| = |(e─f)|.\n\nNow, let's continue with the formal theorem:\n\n```tactics\n  (h5 : |(b─c)| = |(e─f)|) : \n```\n\nSo now we have angle equalities and side equalities.\n\nThe conclusion should be:\n- remaining sides equal: ab = de and ac = df\n- remaining angle equal: angle at a in triangle abc = angle at d in triangle def.\n\nSo in Lean:\n- AB is the line between a and b, so length is |(a─b)|\n- DE is the line between d and e, so length is |(d─e)|\n- AC is the line between a and c, so length is |(a─c)|\n- DF is the line between d and f, so length is |(d─f)|\n- angle at a in triangle abc is angle c-a-b\n- angle at d in triangle def is angle f-d-e\n\nSo:\n\n```tactics\n |(a─b)| = |(d─e)| ∧ |(a─c)| = |(d─f)| ∧ (∠ c:a:b : ℝ) = (∠ f:d:e : ℝ) := by \n```\n\nNow we need to complete the theorem with `by sorry`.\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\n```tactics\ntheorem example_thm (a b c d e f : Point) (AB BC AC DE EF DF : Line) \n  (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) (h2 : formTriangle d e f DE EF DF) \n  (h3 : (∠ a:b:c : ℝ) = (∠ d:e:f : ℝ)) \n  (h4 : (∠ b:c:a : ℝ) = (∠ e:f:d : ℝ)) \n  (h5 : |(b─c)| = |(e─f)|) : \n  |(a─b)| = |(d─e)| ∧ |(a─c)| = |(d─f)| ∧ (∠ c:a:b : ℝ) = (∠ f:d:e : ℝ) := by sorry\n```\n\nWait, this theorem does not exactly match the given example in the statement.\n\nWait, the original given example said:\n- angle ABC = angle DEF\n- angle BCA = angle EFD\n- side BC = side EF\n\nSo that's angle at b, angle at c, and side bc.\nSo in Lean, angle at b is ∠ a:b:c\nangle at c is ∠ b:c:a\nside bc is |(b─c)|\n\nSimilarly in triangle def:\nangle at e is ∠ d:e:f\nangle at f is ∠ e:f:d\nside ef is |(e─f)|\n\nSo the theorem should be:\n\n```tactics\ntheorem example_thm (a b c d e f : Point) (AB BC AC DE EF DF : Line) \n  (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) (h2 : formTriangle d e f DE EF DF) \n  (h3 : (∠ a:b:c : ℝ) = (∠ d:e:f : ℝ)) \n  (h4 : (∠ b:c:a : ℝ) = (∠ e:f:d : ℝ)) \n  (h5 : |(b─c)| = |(e─f)|) : \n  |(a─b)| = |(d─e)| ∧ |(a─c)| = |(d─f)|